1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,200
Every day... 800 million times.

2
00:00:04,433 --> 00:00:10,200
Someone buys a cup of tea. Someone fills up on petrol. Someone sends the rent.

3
00:00:10,433 --> 00:00:14,833
One QR code. One scan. And in 2 seconds, the money is there.

4
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,833
No fee. No charge. Completely free.

5
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:22,433
But the world's largest real-time payment system... does not run for free.

6
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:26,100
Someone is paying its bill. Every single day.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,733
The question is... who?

8
00:00:39,933 --> 00:00:42,100
Every magic trick has three parts.

9
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:46,200
Part one: the Pledge. The magician shows you something ordinary.

10
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:52,667
In our story, that ordinary thing is... this.

11
00:00:55,467 --> 00:01:02,567
August 2016. NPCI's new system, UPI, goes live for the public with 21 banks.

12
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:08,233
In its first full month, it doesn't even reach 1 million transactions.

13
00:01:09,933 --> 00:01:17,933
Then comes 1 January 2020. The government sets the MDR on UPI and RuPay debit cards to zero.

14
00:01:18,267 --> 00:01:27,667
MDR, the merchant discount rate, is the small fee a shop pays to accept a digital payment. On UPI, it used to be up to 0.30%.

15
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:31,233
And then... UPI explodes.

16
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:40,767
September 2026: 24 billion transactions in a single month. More than 800 million, every day.

17
00:01:41,067 --> 00:01:46,300
According to ACI Worldwide, almost half of the world's real-time payments happen in India.

18
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:52,200
The pledge was kept. UPI was free. Fast. Everywhere.

19
00:01:52,533 --> 00:01:55,800
But in magic, what you see... is never the whole truth.

20
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:07,467
Part two: the Turn. The magician makes the ordinary thing... disappear.

21
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:10,500
In UPI, what disappeared... was the bill.

22
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:20,300
Behind every scan runs an entire machine: your app, your bank, NPCI, and the shop's bank.

23
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:24,533
Servers. Security. Fraud checks. Around the clock.

24
00:02:24,867 --> 00:02:29,333
None of it is free. So when the fee went to zero... where did the cost go?

25
00:02:31,467 --> 00:02:35,800
Answer number one: the government. In other words, taxpayers.

26
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:48,833
According to PIB, the government paid banks incentives of ₹1,389 crore in FY22, rising to ₹3,631 crore by FY24.

27
00:02:50,467 --> 00:03:03,333
But the industry kept saying it wasn't enough. The Payments Council of India said running UPI costs about ₹10,000 crore a year, against government support of just ₹1,500 crore.

28
00:03:04,467 --> 00:03:07,867
And all this time, the government's answer was the same.

29
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:17,867
August 2022. The Finance Ministry: “UPI is a digital public good… There is no consideration in Government to levy any charges.”

30
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:25,400
June 2025. Reports of MDR on UPI? “Completely false, baseless and misleading.”

31
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:33,900
Then, just six weeks later, the RBI Governor said: “Costs will have to be paid. Someone will have to bear the cost.”

32
00:03:36,467 --> 00:03:41,200
And in the latest Union Budget, that support was cut by about 10%.

33
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:45,133
The bill hadn't vanished. It just wasn't visible.

34
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:54,800
August 2026. Parliament amends India's payments law.

35
00:03:55,933 --> 00:04:02,000
14 September: the Finance Ministry's notification. The next day, the full rulebook.

36
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:04,367
The rule is simple.

37
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:09,667
Merchant payments above ₹2,000 carry a 0.4% MDR.

38
00:04:09,967 --> 00:04:13,600
Even the biggest payment pays no more than ₹300.

39
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:19,300
Railways, telecom, insurance and fuel: a flat ₹5.

40
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:27,133
Person-to-person transfers stay free. And small shops receiving up to ₹1 lakh a month through UPI pay nothing.

41
00:04:27,467 --> 00:04:31,733
And the key point: the fee cannot be charged to the customer. The shop pays it.

42
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:40,133
NPCI estimates it could raise up to ₹20,600 crore a year.

43
00:04:41,933 --> 00:04:44,300
And then... the countdown began.

44
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:53,000
In a LocalCircles survey of 32,000+ merchants, only 17% said they would absorb the fee themselves.

45
00:04:53,333 --> 00:05:01,433
And of 67,000+ users, only 14% said they would stick with UPI if the fee were passed on to them.

46
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:14,200
Petrol pump dealers in Madhya Pradesh announced that from 16 October, they would stop taking UPI above ₹2,000. Dealers in Punjab and Haryana said the same.

47
00:05:15,467 --> 00:05:23,867
The case reached the Supreme Court. It refused to pause the fee, and asked the government, the RBI and NPCI to respond.

48
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:29,100
And the government's counsel told the court: “It is neither a tax nor a fee.”

49
00:05:31,467 --> 00:05:34,900
Then, just two days before the deadline, NPCI announced:

50
00:05:35,367 --> 00:05:40,033
The MDR would wait until after Diwali. The new date? Not yet decided.

51
00:05:45,933 --> 00:05:51,767
Part three: the Prestige. The moment the vanished thing comes back.

52
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:56,567
The bill that disappeared in 2020... never really disappeared.

53
00:05:57,367 --> 00:06:05,700
Part of it was paid with taxpayers' money — in other words, your money. The rest was carried by banks and payment companies.

54
00:06:06,067 --> 00:06:09,900
And now, the bill has come back — to the shopkeeper's counter.

55
00:06:11,933 --> 00:06:20,200
The law says the charge cannot be passed on to you. But every cost a business carries shows up somewhere, eventually, in prices.

56
00:06:20,533 --> 00:06:30,000
So, who pays for free UPI? As a taxpayer: you. As a customer: maybe you. And if the system weakens, the loss is yours too.

57
00:06:32,467 --> 00:06:38,167
The real question isn't whether UPI should stay free. It's who should pay for this system, and how.

58
00:06:38,467 --> 00:06:47,467
On one side, a system that needs money to run. On the other, millions of small shopkeepers, and the risk of a return to cash.

59
00:06:49,467 --> 00:06:54,533
For now, we wait for the new date. And the case is still pending in the Supreme Court.

60
00:06:54,833 --> 00:06:56,933
The coin... is still spinning.

61
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:10,467
What do you think? Is this fee on payments above ₹2,000 fair, or not? Tell us in the comments. All the sources are in the description.
